1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,600
Imagine you had a computer
program containing

2
00:00:02,680 --> 00:00:05,030
three billion characters of instructions.

3
00:00:06,064 --> 00:00:08,324
Now imagine that every
time you created a new

4
00:00:08,404 --> 00:00:11,874
process, you copied the
entire program into it.

5
00:00:12,517 --> 00:00:13,717
Twice.

6
00:00:15,047 --> 00:00:20,807
Not just the functions that process
needs. The entire thing. Every module.

7
00:00:20,887 --> 00:00:24,897
Every dependency. Every
piece of ancient legacy code.

8
00:00:25,688 --> 00:00:29,178
And then the process simply
ignored ninety-nine percent of it.

9
00:00:29,948 --> 00:00:31,978
That would be terrible
software engineering.

10
00:00:32,954 --> 00:00:34,154
And yet...

11
00:00:34,729 --> 00:00:36,839
...that's more or less what your cells do.

12
00:00:37,583 --> 00:00:40,763
Almost every cell in your body
that has a nucleus contains

13
00:00:40,843 --> 00:00:43,873
essentially the same
complete copy of your genome.

14
00:00:44,677 --> 00:00:47,787
A neuron has it. A liver cell has it.

15
00:00:48,077 --> 00:00:49,577
A skin cell has it.

16
00:00:49,657 --> 00:00:52,267
And yet they behave
completely differently.

17
00:00:53,163 --> 00:00:56,853
The code is there. Most of
it simply isn't being used.

18
00:00:57,677 --> 00:01:01,427
A gene can be thought of, very
roughly, as a function or a module.

19
00:01:02,295 --> 00:01:04,975
It contains information that
can ultimately be used to

20
00:01:05,055 --> 00:01:08,685
produce a particular functional
product, often a protein.

21
00:01:09,635 --> 00:01:11,805
So we can imagine something like this.

22
00:01:12,505 --> 00:01:15,455
But having the function in the
code doesn't mean it is running.

23
00:01:16,265 --> 00:01:18,095
Something has to activate it.

24
00:01:18,665 --> 00:01:20,815
And that's where biology gets interesting.

25
00:01:21,864 --> 00:01:24,504
Because in a computer,
I can simply write this.

26
00:01:24,584 --> 00:01:26,514
Or in machine code.

27
00:01:27,338 --> 00:01:31,548
The processor knows exactly what
to do. There is an instruction.

28
00:01:32,118 --> 00:01:34,788
There is an address. There
is an instruction pointer.

29
00:01:35,715 --> 00:01:38,415
But a cell doesn't have
a CPU sitting there

30
00:01:38,495 --> 00:01:41,405
executing DNA instructions one by one.

31
00:01:42,062 --> 00:01:43,262
So...

32
00:01:43,673 --> 00:01:45,263
...how does it know what to use?

33
00:01:47,933 --> 00:01:51,043
This is where the computer
analogy starts getting weird.

34
00:01:51,973 --> 00:01:55,323
In a computer, you can jump
to a specific memory address.

35
00:01:56,201 --> 00:01:59,931
You can have access permissions.
You can have processes.

36
00:02:00,881 --> 00:02:02,751
You can have configuration files.

37
00:02:03,261 --> 00:02:06,151
You can have events triggering
specific pieces of code.

38
00:02:06,925 --> 00:02:10,255
Biology has things that play
surprisingly similar roles.

39
00:02:11,096 --> 00:02:13,636
Transcription factors can
bind to particular DNA

40
00:02:13,716 --> 00:02:17,906
sequences and influence
whether a gene is transcribed.

41
00:02:18,573 --> 00:02:23,483
Regulatory DNA sequences can affect
when and where genes are expressed.

42
00:02:24,613 --> 00:02:29,703
The structure of chromatin affects how
accessible particular regions of DNA are.

43
00:02:30,383 --> 00:02:33,893
And cells constantly receive
signals from their environment.

44
00:02:34,725 --> 00:02:39,495
So instead of a CPU, an instruction
pointer, a memory address, and execute...

45
00:02:40,431 --> 00:02:42,301
...you have something more like this.

46
00:02:43,130 --> 00:02:45,220
There isn't one instruction pointer.

47
00:02:45,930 --> 00:02:48,920
There is an enormous network
of molecular interactions.

48
00:02:49,905 --> 00:02:51,335
The DNA is there.

49
00:02:51,785 --> 00:02:54,125
But different parts of it
are more or less accessible

50
00:02:54,205 --> 00:02:56,855
to the molecular
machinery that reads them.

51
00:02:57,555 --> 00:03:01,145
So, having the code is not the
same as having access to the code.

52
00:03:01,815 --> 00:03:05,345
A liver cell doesn't need to delete
all the neuron related genes.

53
00:03:06,215 --> 00:03:08,585
It can simply regulate
whether they're used.

54
00:03:10,975 --> 00:03:13,205
Let's go back to our software project.

55
00:03:13,995 --> 00:03:16,465
Suppose you're developing
a huge application.

56
00:03:17,495 --> 00:03:19,665
You create a new module called finger.

57
00:03:19,975 --> 00:03:23,085
And instead of importing the
few functions it needs...

58
00:03:23,993 --> 00:03:27,123
...you copy the entire
human repository into it.

59
00:03:27,758 --> 00:03:31,598
The brain. The liver. The heart. The
immune system. The reproductive system.

60
00:03:31,678 --> 00:03:32,878
Everything.

61
00:03:33,123 --> 00:03:34,473
And you do this again...

62
00:03:35,517 --> 00:03:36,717
...for every cell.

63
00:03:38,297 --> 00:03:42,207
As a programmer, you would
probably ask... Who designed this?

64
00:03:42,988 --> 00:03:44,478
And the answer is...

65
00:03:44,948 --> 00:03:46,148
Nobody.

66
00:03:47,543 --> 00:03:49,473
Well... Not nobody. Evolution.

67
00:03:50,456 --> 00:03:53,506
And evolution has a very
different development philosophy.

68
00:03:56,366 --> 00:03:59,406
Imagine opening a repository
called human, with a

69
00:03:59,486 --> 00:04:02,176
commit history stretching
back billions of years.

70
00:04:03,033 --> 00:04:07,043
There was no architect sitting down
and designing version one point zero.

71
00:04:07,433 --> 00:04:09,203
There was no clean rewrite.

72
00:04:09,813 --> 00:04:13,363
There was no human version two
with a redesigned architecture.

73
00:04:14,269 --> 00:04:18,389
Instead... Add feature.
Patch bug. Duplicate module.

74
00:04:18,469 --> 00:04:19,999
Reuse old module.

75
00:04:20,854 --> 00:04:24,754
Work around limitation.
Add another workaround.

76
00:04:25,174 --> 00:04:28,264
Keep old API because
something depends on it.

77
00:04:28,975 --> 00:04:30,795
Again. And again. And again.

78
00:04:30,875 --> 00:04:32,565
For billions of years.

79
00:04:33,404 --> 00:04:36,694
Evolution doesn't ask what
the cleanest architecture is.

80
00:04:37,444 --> 00:04:39,334
It asks a much simpler question.

81
00:04:40,204 --> 00:04:42,514
Does this organism reproduce successfully?

82
00:04:43,488 --> 00:04:45,658
If the answer is yes, the code survives.

83
00:04:48,278 --> 00:04:51,338
Imagine inheriting a software
project that has been maintained

84
00:04:51,418 --> 00:04:55,698
by millions of programmers,
over billions of years, with

85
00:04:55,778 --> 00:04:59,148
absolutely no possibility of
rewriting the whole thing.

86
00:04:59,851 --> 00:05:01,661
You discover something and ask...

87
00:05:01,851 --> 00:05:03,421
Why does this exist?

88
00:05:04,131 --> 00:05:05,881
Because something depended on it.

89
00:05:06,593 --> 00:05:09,943
Why is this API so weird? Legacy.

90
00:05:10,872 --> 00:05:16,662
That is evolution. It is the ultimate
legacy system. It doesn't refactor.

91
00:05:16,752 --> 00:05:18,642
It modifies what already exists.

92
00:05:19,591 --> 00:05:21,461
And after billions of years...

93
00:05:22,353 --> 00:05:23,553
...you get us.

94
00:05:26,137 --> 00:05:31,307
Modern software development has a
fairly sensible idea. You make a change.

95
00:05:31,997 --> 00:05:33,197
Then you test it.

96
00:05:33,768 --> 00:05:35,028
Commit. Build. Unit tests.

97
00:05:35,108 --> 00:05:37,878
Integration tests. Deployment.

98
00:05:38,837 --> 00:05:43,587
Biology has something that looks
vaguely similar. A mutation occurs.

99
00:05:44,567 --> 00:05:47,907
And then... The organism
either survives and reproduces.

100
00:05:47,987 --> 00:05:49,537
Or it doesn't.

101
00:05:50,169 --> 00:05:52,099
There is no staging environment.

102
00:05:52,609 --> 00:05:54,019
There is no rollback.

103
00:05:54,789 --> 00:05:57,339
And occasionally something
much worse happens.

104
00:05:58,309 --> 00:06:00,069
A mutation can make a cell better at

105
00:06:00,149 --> 00:06:02,859
surviving and reproducing
inside the organism.

106
00:06:03,909 --> 00:06:05,439
That can contribute to cancer.

107
00:06:06,388 --> 00:06:10,518
So biology has a rather terrifying
version of continuous integration.

108
00:06:11,588 --> 00:06:14,098
The tests are performed
on the production system.

109
00:06:16,978 --> 00:06:20,608
But this is where the software
analogy becomes even more interesting.

110
00:06:21,758 --> 00:06:25,868
When you look at the human body, it's
tempting to imagine something like this.

111
00:06:26,591 --> 00:06:29,001
But that's not really
how the system works.

112
00:06:29,671 --> 00:06:33,141
There is no central server sending
instructions to every cell.

113
00:06:33,857 --> 00:06:36,927
Your cells constantly monitor
their local environment.

114
00:06:37,777 --> 00:06:39,507
They receive chemical signals.

115
00:06:40,237 --> 00:06:44,267
They interact with neighbouring
cells. They respond to hormones.

116
00:06:45,201 --> 00:06:47,491
A cell doesn't receive a message saying...

117
00:06:48,305 --> 00:06:50,676
...cell number seven
million, three hundred and

118
00:06:50,756 --> 00:06:53,285
ninety-two thousand, one
hundred and eighty-four.

119
00:06:53,365 --> 00:06:55,715
Please express gene X now.

120
00:06:57,695 --> 00:07:01,805
If you're a programmer, there's another
concept that suddenly becomes useful.

121
00:07:02,575 --> 00:07:03,805
Distributed systems.

122
00:07:04,919 --> 00:07:08,289
Your cells are effectively enormous
numbers of individual agents.

123
00:07:09,399 --> 00:07:10,969
Each one has its own state.

124
00:07:11,279 --> 00:07:16,229
Its own sensors. Its own
inputs. Its own responses.

125
00:07:17,311 --> 00:07:20,441
There isn't one giant loop
telling every cell what to do.

126
00:07:21,251 --> 00:07:23,681
That's closer to a multi-agent system.

127
00:07:23,771 --> 00:07:25,261
Or a swarm.

128
00:07:25,571 --> 00:07:27,581
Or a cellular automaton.

129
00:07:28,357 --> 00:07:29,887
Simple local rules...

130
00:07:30,888 --> 00:07:34,158
...producing astonishingly
complicated global behaviour.

131
00:07:36,938 --> 00:07:40,288
Here's another thing that would be
unacceptable in normal software.

132
00:07:41,258 --> 00:07:43,968
Your body has been running
continuously for decades.

133
00:07:44,951 --> 00:07:47,801
No reboot. No scheduled
maintenance window.

134
00:07:48,545 --> 00:07:50,305
And yet the system is constantly changing.

135
00:07:50,385 --> 00:07:52,105
Cells die.

136
00:07:52,185 --> 00:07:54,225
New cells are created.

137
00:07:54,305 --> 00:07:57,175
Proteins are produced and destroyed.

138
00:07:58,141 --> 00:08:00,841
DNA is copied. Damage is repaired.

139
00:08:00,921 --> 00:08:02,671
Components are replaced.

140
00:08:03,636 --> 00:08:06,866
The system doesn't shut down
so that maintenance can happen.

141
00:08:07,576 --> 00:08:09,546
It performs maintenance while running.

142
00:08:10,326 --> 00:08:12,506
It's almost as if you had
a server that could replace

143
00:08:12,586 --> 00:08:16,016
parts of itself while the server
was processing requests...

144
00:08:17,035 --> 00:08:18,805
...without ever going offline.

145
00:08:19,755 --> 00:08:22,975
Cells have their own machinery.
They communicate. They respond.

146
00:08:23,055 --> 00:08:27,245
They regulate one another. They
form tissues. Tissues form organs.

147
00:08:27,815 --> 00:08:29,415
Organs form systems.

148
00:08:29,495 --> 00:08:31,065
And systems form an organism.

149
00:08:31,909 --> 00:08:36,239
And there is no single component holding
the entire architecture in its head.

150
00:08:39,159 --> 00:08:40,359
And somehow...

151
00:08:41,175 --> 00:08:42,375
...it works.

152
00:08:43,552 --> 00:08:45,782
Not perfectly. Far from perfectly.

153
00:08:46,699 --> 00:08:48,299
But well enough to build eyes.

154
00:08:48,379 --> 00:08:50,889
Hearts. Immune systems. Brains.

155
00:08:51,895 --> 00:08:54,455
And organisms capable
of writing software to

156
00:08:54,535 --> 00:08:57,365
complain about how badly
the system is designed.

157
00:09:00,685 --> 00:09:02,435
We've been calling DNA code.

158
00:09:03,165 --> 00:09:04,675
And the analogy is useful.

159
00:09:05,385 --> 00:09:07,195
Genes look a bit like functions.

160
00:09:08,065 --> 00:09:11,055
Mutations look a bit like
changes to source code.

161
00:09:11,795 --> 00:09:13,555
But there is an important difference.

162
00:09:13,635 --> 00:09:17,585
DNA isn't actually a complete
blueprint for a human.

163
00:09:18,315 --> 00:09:20,155
There isn't literally a line saying...

164
00:09:20,235 --> 00:09:23,445
Create arm. Create eye. Create heart.

165
00:09:24,265 --> 00:09:27,265
Instead, DNA participates
in a gigantic system of

166
00:09:27,345 --> 00:09:30,505
interactions involving
molecules, cells, physical

167
00:09:30,585 --> 00:09:34,275
forces, chemical gradients,
timing and environment.

168
00:09:35,347 --> 00:09:37,797
The organism emerges from all of this.

169
00:09:38,579 --> 00:09:41,339
So DNA is perhaps better
thought of as a set of

170
00:09:41,419 --> 00:09:44,889
instructions and rules embedded
in a physical system...

171
00:09:45,750 --> 00:09:48,620
...rather than a complete
source code listing for a human.

172
00:09:49,250 --> 00:09:51,000
And that's actually much stranger.

173
00:09:53,690 --> 00:09:58,080
We can sequence a human genome,
identify genes and measure proteins.

174
00:09:59,055 --> 00:10:01,315
But we still can't take
the DNA from one ordinary

175
00:10:01,395 --> 00:10:04,985
human cell, put it into
a computer, press run...

176
00:10:05,701 --> 00:10:07,531
...and watch that human develop.

177
00:10:08,506 --> 00:10:12,116
We have the genome. But the
genome isn't the whole machine.

178
00:10:15,446 --> 00:10:16,896
And that's where we end up.

179
00:10:17,606 --> 00:10:19,656
We started with a familiar idea.

180
00:10:20,246 --> 00:10:22,256
DNA is like computer code.

181
00:10:23,055 --> 00:10:25,965
And the analogy turns out
to be surprisingly useful.

182
00:10:26,855 --> 00:10:29,805
But the deeper we look, the
stranger the system becomes.

183
00:10:30,705 --> 00:10:34,455
There is no CPU. There
is no operating system.

184
00:10:34,945 --> 00:10:36,555
There is no central server.

185
00:10:37,291 --> 00:10:39,731
There is no programmer.
There is no GitHub.

186
00:10:39,811 --> 00:10:41,771
There is no code review.

187
00:10:41,851 --> 00:10:43,501
There is no staging environment.

188
00:10:43,791 --> 00:10:45,671
There is no rollback.

189
00:10:45,751 --> 00:10:48,321
There is barely even a clean architecture.

190
00:10:49,190 --> 00:10:52,010
There are trillions of
tiny agents, each following

191
00:10:52,090 --> 00:10:54,840
local rules, communicating
with their neighbours,

192
00:10:55,686 --> 00:10:59,366
using a gigantic, ancient,
continuously modified repository...

193
00:10:59,446 --> 00:11:03,016
And somehow producing
a functioning organism.

194
00:11:03,919 --> 00:11:05,729
So if biology is software...

195
00:11:06,767 --> 00:11:11,797
...it may be the oldest, messiest, most
overengineered legacy system imaginable.

196
00:11:12,687 --> 00:11:15,037
A system with billions
of years of commits.

197
00:11:15,747 --> 00:11:17,427
Almost no refactoring.

198
00:11:17,507 --> 00:11:19,757
No central administrator.

199
00:11:20,507 --> 00:11:22,117
And absolutely no reboot.

200
00:11:22,965 --> 00:11:24,165
And somehow...

201
00:11:24,981 --> 00:11:27,771
...it's been running in
production for billions of years.
